Declare the tessellation stages' layouts on the GLSL target.

GLSL requires a tessellation control shader to declare its output control point count,
`layout(vertices = N) out;`, and a tessellation evaluation shader its abstract patch,
`layout(triangles) in;` (or quads, or isolines). HLSL carries the two as `[outputcontrolpoints(N)]`
on the hull shader and `[domain(...)]` on the domain shader, which Slang keeps as
OutputControlPointsDecoration and DomainDecoration. The SPIR-V emitter turns them into the
OutputVertices and Triangles execution modes; the GLSL emitter
(GLSLSourceEmitter::emitEntryPointAttributesImpl, source/slang/slang-emit-glsl.cpp) has no case for
either stage and declares neither. glslang refuses both stages: "At least one shader must specify
an output layout(vertices=...)" and "At least one shader must specify an input layout primitive".

This declares both. The spacing and the vertex order, which GLSL also declares on the evaluation
shader, are attributes of the hull shader in HLSL and are not known to a domain shader compiled on
its own, so GLSL's defaults apply there. A regression test is added as
tools/slang-unit-test/unit-test-glsl-tessellation-layouts.cpp.

Measured on v2026.21 (win-x64): glslangValidator -V --target-env vulkan1.2 refused the GLSL of a hull
and a domain shader for the two missing layouts, once their other errors were patched.
